 ['s6-deck', `Diego's deck (sessions/session-6/deck/index.html, PR #20 2026-11-11, PR #21 2026-11-23)
Dark terminal design, speaker notes as HTML comments, runs on the kurasu slides API. Built from Flavio Copes's "Open-weight AI models", one slide per section:
• What weights are (a layer as a matrix multiplication) · architecture vs weights · what open-weight means · open access / open weights / open source · the training recipe
• Downloading a local model (GGUF, Ollama, llama.cpp, MLX) · quantization
• What open weights allow: pin the version, data on your machine, pick who runs it, fine-tune, study it, small models as parts
• Licenses · recent large releases · why release large models (inference companies, researchers, hardware makers, framework teams, quantization experts) · what they don't guarantee · how to use one · 10 evaluation checks · takeaways
• Demos: ollama run qwen3.5:4b on the laptop · the same model in Pi through an "ollama" provider in models.json + /model · Hugging Face official vs community GGUFs (ollama run hf.co/…)
• Real case: Qwen3.8-27B-pi-adapted, a LoRA trained with RL inside Pi; pass@1 under Pi 39.2% → 64.7% SWE-bench Pro, 39.5% → 55.8% Terminal-Bench 2.1 (model card). Its notes tie it to Session 5: "el harness es una pieza, y el modelo se puede entrenar para esa pieza".`],
