const ansPD = `Answer: what pi-durable is, against pi-coding-agent

Pi-durable is Earendil's experimental framework for long-running agents on any JavaScript runtime. Its loop does not run in a terminal process that one person drives: it runs in whatever process owns a storage, and every step is committed state that survives a crash. One correction to the framing in decision 8: the announcement says it "does not replace the Pi coding agent". It is a framework to build agent applications, coding agents included, and its demos borrow the coding agent's TUI. Lessons are meant to flow back into Pi.

It sits on the same pi-ai as the coding agent but does not use pi-agent-core: it has its own Harness, task scheduler and storage layer.`;
const factsPD = `Facts (Earendil's announcement, README and Cloudflare sample, as read by the researcher; no trial run)
• Version 1.6.0 (2026-11-20), MIT, ~15k lines; the README says "Experimental. The API changes without notice between releases."
• Terms: harness (storage + machinery for parallel conversations), conversation (immutable entries), agent (model + settings + tools), task (everything it runs), execution environment (where tools act).
• State: atomic commits write entries, documents (typed JSON next to the transcript) and tasks together. Storage is pluggable: memory, IndexedDB, SQLite, JSONL, Cloudflare Durable Object. One process owns a storage; others attach read-only.
• Crash: each task is a checkpointed state machine; a new process calls resume(). A cut-off model request is re-sent (costs tokens); a cut-off tool reruns only with replay: "safe", otherwise the model is told it was interrupted. requestId makes a submission exactly-once.
• Tools: CodingTools (read, write, edit, bash) plus code mode (QuickJS) and tool search. The env function runs per tool call, so tools can act in a local dir, a container, a remote VM or a sandbox while the harness lives elsewhere.
• Clients: watch() sends a view, then each commit as small operations over a socket you wire yourself; any number of clients attach, late joiners start from the current view, any of them steers (whenBusy: steer / followUp / reject).
• Background: tasks and conversations form one ownership tree; background tasks survive an abort; sleepUntil holds no process while waiting. Subagents aren't built in: a tool creates a child conversation it owns.
• Extensions: named bundles of tools, prompt sections, hooks, tasks and documents; conversations store names, not code; hot-replaceable. No permission model documented; the announcement shows an approval hook as an extension.
• Cloudflare sample: one Durable Object per session holds the harness over DO SQLite; DO alarms wake it (onWake); hibernating WebSockets per conversation; forks, scheduled follow-ups, a search subagent. No container or sandbox in the sample.
• Stated costs: experimental API, single-owner storage, re-sent requests, tools must be written with replay in mind, SQLite may lose the newest commits on host failure. Not documented: latency, security model, infra cost.
• Your vault notes add: lessons flow back into the coding agent; Armin and Mario discuss it on the Fragmented podcast (episode 312, in your watch queue).`;
const tentPD = `Reference only, never on the slides (decision 8): how tentacles uses it (code scout, with file references in its report)
• One Durable Object per session runs Harness.open over the DO's SQLite; the DO alarm calls the harness's wake, so runs resume after eviction.
• Tools: pi-durable's CodingTools and code mode; files live in the DO's SQLite, exec runs on an isolate shell or a workspace container; reads are replay-safe, write/edit/delete/exec are not.
• The parent (pi or Claude Code plugin) reaches it over HTTP + one WebSocket per session: spawn, prompt (steer or follow-up), answer, messages, close. A question waits inside the run and survives evictions.
• Costs its docs name: nothing runs locally; skills and context fixed at spawn; container cold start ~1–3 s; npm install 11.4 s vs 4.8 s on the container disk; a container stop loses processes outside /workspace; unpushed work goes with the session after a month.`;
